---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro'
import { playableGames } from '../../lib/games.ts'
import { REPO } from '../../lib/site.ts'

const games = playableGames().length
---

<Page
  title="Mortar's mission: free, no account, open source"
  description="Mortar is free, needs no account, collects nothing about you and is open source under the AGPL-3.0. Why it is built that way."
  heading="Free, no account, open source."
  kicker="Mission"
  lede="Mortar is a mod manager for the people who play the games. It costs nothing, asks you for nothing, and anyone can read its source."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Mission', href: '/mission/' }]}
>
  <h2>Free</h2>
  <p>Mortar costs nothing and has no paid tier. There are no ads in the app. Every one of the {games} games it supports gets the same features.</p>

  <h2>No account</h2>
  <p>You don't sign up for Mortar. It has no account system and no server that collects anything about you. A mod site that needs your own account, such as Nexus Mods, gets your key from you, and Mortar keeps it in your system keyring.</p>
  <p>Profiles move between your computers without an account too: pair two computers with a short code, or point Mortar at a folder your sync tool already carries.</p>

  <h2>Nothing collected</h2>
  <p>Mortar has no analytics and no telemetry. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> lists every service it talks to, when, and what each one receives. A share link carries your profile after the <code>#</code>, which browsers never send to a server.</p>

  <h2>Mods come from their makers</h2>
  <p>Every mod downloads from its own page on its own site, so the people who make mods keep their downloads, their pages and their support links. Mortar never re-hosts a mod file. <a href="/sources/">The mod sites</a></p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>Mortar is licensed under the <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html">AGPL-3.0</a>. The whole source is on <a href={REPO}>GitHub</a>. GitHub Actions builds each release from it, and you can check every file it ships against a signature. <a href="/code-signing/">Code signing policy</a></p>

  <div class="cta" style="margin-top:40px">
    <a class="btn primary" href="/download/">Download Mortar</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href={REPO}>Read the source</a>
  </div>
</Page>
